│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_serial_trie_reorder                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The original program was deeply broken: it used exponential per-row tuple recursion with  │
│                          │ ThreadPoolExecutors, mutated rows via `result_df.loc` with unordered futures, returned an │
│                          │ empty `column_orderings` list (violating the required API), asserted invariants that fail │
│                          │ on real data, and even printed debug output. The validation failures stem from            │
│                          │ invalid/missing per-row column orderings and data-shape/identity corruption during the    │
│                          │ recursive `pd.DataFrame` concatenations.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a correct, bounded algorithm aligned with  │
│                          │ the actual objective (character-Trie prefix reuse):                                       │
│                          │ 1. Serialize every cell once (missing → ""), reusing the arrays across candidates.        │
│                          │ 2. Build at most three cheap candidate per-row field orderings: (a) a global              │
│                          │ frequency-ranked order scored by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), (b) a deterministic         │
│                          │ length-ranked order, (c) a conditional partition tree that recursively picks the field    │
│                          │ with the highest length-weighted repetition inside each row group and partitions on its   │
│                          │ serialized values, with a deterministic global-order tail (bounded depth/node count).     │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the exact serial-Trie objective (sum of LCPs of              │
│                          │ lexicographically adjacent serialized rows), using C-speed binary-search prefix           │
│                          │ comparisons instead of per-character Python loops.                                        │
│                          │ 4. Emit the best candidate: the returned DataFrame contains every original cell value     │
│                          │ permuted per row (object dtype, original index, original column names), with a per-row    │
│                          │ list of column names consistent with the data. `col_merge` is honored via the parent      │
│                          │ `merging_columns`; `row_stop`/`col_stop` bound tree depth. No debug prints, no data       │
│                          │ fabrication, no evaluator state access.                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0184                                                                                   │
