│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for multiple correctness reasons: it returned an   │
│                          │ empty `column_orderings` list (`[]`), sorted/fabricated rows via `original_index` merging │
│                          │ and `sort_values` (destroying row identity relative to returned orderings), could         │
│                          │ mutate/duplicate cells through recursive DataFrame rebuilding with                        │
│                          │ `result_df.update`/concat, and its prints/unused dependency paths made behavior           │
│                          │ nondeterministic.                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the editable algorithm with a clean, correct one that:                   │
│                          │ 1. Preserves every cell, row, and column exactly (object dtype output, no                 │
│                          │ padding/dropping).                                                                        │
│                          │ 2. Builds up to three cheap global column-ordering candidates (length-weighted            │
│                          │ pair-repetition ranking `len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1)`, its reverse-variation, and original order), │
│                          │ plus contiguity constraints for `col_merge`.                                              │
│                          │ 3. Scores each candidate with the true serial Trie objective: sum of adjacent LCPs of the │
│                          │ sorted serialized rows ("".join of fillna("") str values), using a C-speed slice-based    │
│                          │ LCP.                                                                                      │
│                          │ 4. Returns the best-scoring candidate with a valid per-row column ordering list of length │
│                          │ len(df).                                                                                  │
