│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_trie_prefix_optimized_reorder                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The original program was broken in several fundamental ways: (1) it returned an empty     │
│                          │ list `[]` as `column_orderings`, so per-row column orderings were invalid/missing; (2) it │
│                          │ fabricated an "original_index" helper column, merged/sorted rows with pandas merge (which │
│                          │ can reorder/duplicate rows and coerce dtypes), and its recursive path reconstructed       │
│                          │ DataFrames from value lists, altering dtypes and row identity; (3) its recursion used     │
│                          │ heuristics (squared string lengths, `itertuples` attribute access on arbitrary labels)    │
│                          │ that frequently crashed or diverged from the actual objective (character-Trie prefix      │
│                          │ reuse).                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a correct, bounded implementation aligned  │
│                          │ with the real objective: serialize cells (missing → ""), build a small set of candidate   │
│                          │ per-row column orderings — (a) global ordering ranked by sum                              │
│                          │ len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1), (b) frequency-ranked ordering, and (c) a bounded            │
│                          │ conditional partition tree that picks a leading field per group by length-weighted pair   │
│                          │ repetition and recurses on partitions — then score each candidate exactly with the ideal  │
│                          │ Trie reuse (sum of adjacent LCPs of the sorted serialized rows, computed via C-speed      │
│                          │ binary search on prefix slices), and output the best. It preserves every cell value and   │
│                          │ the exact number of rows/columns, honors col_merge via the existing merging_columns API,  │
│                          │ enforces one_way_dep precedence by swapping positions, pushes highly distinct columns     │
│                          │ (per distinct_value_threshold) to the tail, and returns a valid per-row                   │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list consistent with the returned object-dtype DataFrame.              │
