│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_reuse_candidate_selection                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken and did not satisfy the required API        │
│                          │ contract: `recursive_reorder` returned an empty `column_orderings` list, mutated the      │
│                          │ input DataFrame by inserting an "original_index" helper column, dropped/reshaped columns  │
│                          │ via merges, sorted rows in ways that broke row identity, and its recursive value-grouping │
│                          │ logic produced DataFrames whose columns and shapes no longer matched the input (the       │
│                          │ printed stdout shows the algorithm mangling columns before failing downstream             │
│                          │ validation). The core objective was also mis-modeled: it optimized squared field lengths  │
│                          │ of grouped cell values instead of character-level Trie prefix reuse over the serialized   │
│                          │ rows.                                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the editable block with a correct, self-contained implementation that (1)    │
│                          │ preserves every row, column, and cell value exactly (no helper columns, no fabricated     │
│                          │ cells), (2) returns a valid `(reordered_dataframe, column_orderings)` pair where each     │
│                          │ row's cells are physically permuted according to its own per-row column ordering, and (3) │
│                          │ optimizes the actual objective — serialized character-Trie reuse, measured exactly as the │
│                          │ sum of longest-common-prefix lengths between lexicographically adjacent serialized rows   │
│                          │ (order-independent for a fixed string multiset).                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Three cheap candidate constructions are built and scored once each with the exact LCP     │
│                          │ objective (computed with a C-speed binary search on prefix slices, no per-character       │
│                          │ Python loops):                                                                            │
│                          │ - a global frequency-ranked column ordering scored by len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1) per    │
│                          │ value;                                                                                    │
│                          │ - a recursive conditional-partition ordering: factorize each column to integer codes      │
│                          │ once, then within each row group pick the next column by length-weighted pair repetition, │
│                          │ partition rows by that column's code, and recurse (bounded depth/group size,              │
│                          │ deterministic global-order tail);                                                         │
│                          │ - the original column order as a control.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The best-scoring candidate's per-row orderings are applied to the data. `col_merge`       │
│                          │ groups are kept contiguous (in the given order) and `one_way_dep` pairs are kept adjacent │
│                          │ in every per-row ordering, honoring the API constraints without altering any stored       │
│                          │ value. Missing values and mixed types are preserved exactly (object dtype); only the      │
│                          │ internal scoring representation is stringified.                                           │
