│ patch_description        │ The program returned an empty list of column orderings and returned a DataFrame whose     │
│                          │ rows were sorted lexicographically as tuples — it permuted cell values across rows rather │
│                          │ than applying per-row column permutations, and it never produced valid per-row column     │
│                          │ orderings consistent with the returned data (required output). The original code also had │
│                          │ fragile attribute-based row handling (`hasattr`/`getattr` on itertuples rows), duplicated │
│                          │ values via `result_df.update(group)`, and stripped/rebuilt columns in ways that altered   │
│                          │ data.                                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a simpler, correct algorithm:              │
│                          │ - Serialize each cell to string once (missing -> ""), keep the original values.           │
│                          │ - Build 2-3 cheap candidate global column orders (frequency-ranked, length-weighted       │
│                          │ repetition-ranked, and a conditional partition tree that groups rows by a chosen field    │
│                          │ value and orders suffixes per group).                                                     │
│                          │ - Score each candidate by computing the exact serial-Trie character reuse = sum of LCPs   │
│                          │ of lexicographically adjacent serialized row strings (binary-search LCP on string slices, │
│                          │ all C-speed).                                                                             │
│                          │ - Apply the best per-row column permutation to build the output DataFrame (object dtype,  │
│                          │ all original values preserved, same shape, no row sorting/merging).                       │
│                          │ - Return (reordered_df, per-row column orderings lists) consistent with the returned      │
│                          │ data.                                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ All public method signatures preserved; data and shape fully preserved; handles missing   │
│                          │ values and mixed types.                                                                   │
