│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_lcp_candidate_orderings_fix                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken with respect to the actual objective and    │
│                          │ the API contract: (1) it returned an empty list as `column_orderings`, giving the         │
│                          │ evaluator no per-row column orderings; (2) it fabricated an `original_index` column,      │
│                          │ merged/sorted/dropped columns, and mutated cell positions, changing the data shape and    │
│                          │ values; (3) it used `itertuples` attribute access (`hasattr(row, col)`) which fails for   │
│                          │ arbitrary/duplicate column names and reordered *values* inside rows, destroying row       │
│                          │ identity; (4) it optimized squared field lengths / adjacent-row grouping, which does not  │
│                          │ match the serial character-Trie reuse objective (sum of LCPs of lexicographically         │
│                          │ adjacent serialized rows).                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: complete rewrite inside the block. The algorithm now serializes each cell exactly as │
│                          │ the scorer does (`""` for missing, else `str(v)`), factorizes column values into integer  │
│                          │ codes once, builds up to three cheap candidate constructions — (a) a global               │
│                          │ frequency–length-ranked column order (metric Σ len(v)·cnt·(cnt−1)), (b) a recursive       │
│                          │ conditional partition tree that picks the best remaining column per group by the same     │
│                          │ length-weighted pair-repetition metric and assigns per-group suffix orders (bounded       │
│                          │ depth, deterministic tail), and (c) a frequency-only global order — scores each candidate │
│                          │ with the true objective (sort serialized rows, sum binary-searched LCPs, all comparisons  │
│                          │ done via C-level prefix-slice equality), and returns the best candidate's per-row column  │
│                          │ orderings. Data is never altered: the returned DataFrame preserves every cell, row, and   │
│                          │ column; `col_merge` is honored via the parent `merging_columns` when provided;            │
│                          │ `one_way_dep` pairs are enforced to keep source before dependent in the orderings. The    │
│                          │ whole body is wrapped so any unexpected failure falls back to returning the original      │
│                          │ DataFrame with identity orderings, guaranteeing all datasets succeed.                     │
