│ patch_name               │ valid_per_row_column_orderings                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The program failed validation because `reorder` returned an empty list `[]` for           │
│                          │ `column_orderings` (see `return final_df, []`), while the evaluator requires valid        │
│                          │ per-row column orderings consistent with the returned data. Additionally, the old code's  │
│                          │ recursive column/row splitting mutated cell placement without producing consistent        │
│                          │ per-row orderings, discarded/re-merged columns with an "original_index" helper column     │
│                          │ (risking shape/dtype corruption), and its `column_recursion` wrote rows into              │
│                          │ `result_df.loc` from `as_completed` (non-deterministic row assignment) and referenced     │
│                          │ `cols_settled` outside its loop.                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the editable block with a simple, correct, bounded algorithm:            │
│                          │ 1. Preserve every row and cell exactly (object dtype for mixed types, no helper columns,  │
│                          │ no discarded columns).                                                                    │
│                          │ 2. Build a global column ordering using the length-weighted repetition heuristic: for     │
│                          │ each column, score = sum over serialized values v of len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1), with   │
│                          │ deterministic tie-breaks (fewer distinct values / longer average / column name).          │
│                          │ 3. Serialize each row with that column order (fillna -> ""), sort rows by the serialized  │
│                          │ string (lexicographic order maximizes adjacent LCP = ideal trie reuse), and rebuild the   │
│                          │ dataframe in that row order with the chosen column order.                                 │
│                          │ 4. Return `column_orderings` as a list with one column-name ordering per output row,      │
│                          │ consistent with the returned dataframe.                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This guarantees a valid (dataframe, orderings) pair, preserves shape and values, and      │
│                          │ improves prefix caching via lexicographic row sorting and repetition-weighted column      │
│                          │ placement.                                                                                │
