│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_prefix_cache_reorder                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed because its recursive reorder pipeline corrupted/reshaped the │
│                          │ DataFrame: it added an "original_index" column, split rows into grouped/remaining halves, │
│                          │ wrote rows into an empty result_df with reset positions (duplicating/dropping row         │
│                          │ identity via pd.merge), applied a final `sort_values` that changed row order, and         │
│                          │ returned an empty `column_orderings` list — so validation of per-row column orderings and │
│                          │ data preservation failed. It also used undefined-parent semantics (merging_columns),      │
│                          │ squared-length scoring (misaligned with the character-Trie objective), and heavy          │
│                          │ ThreadPool recursion that could hang or produce shape mismatches.                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the entire editable block with a correct, bounded algorithm:             │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell exactly as the evaluator does (str(value), missing → "") once,     │
│                          │ into an object matrix.                                                                    │
│                          │ 2. Build at most three cheap candidate per-row column orderings: (a) global               │
│                          │ frequency-ranked order scored by sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), (b) a recursive conditional │
│                          │ partition tree that picks a remaining field by length-weighted pair repetition within     │
│                          │ each group and orders suffixes per group (bounded depth, per-row orders), (c) the         │
│                          │ original column order.                                                                    │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the exact ideal character-Trie reuse (sum of adjacent LCP    │
│                          │ lengths of the sorted serialized rows, computed with a C-speed binary search on prefix    │
│                          │ slices), and return the best.                                                             │
│                          │ 4. Rebuild the output DataFrame with per-row column permutations, preserving every cell   │
│                          │ value and row identity; return matching per-row column orderings. col_merge is honored by │
│                          │ concatenating member columns' serialized values into a single merged column.              │
│                          │ row_stop/col_stop/early_stop/distinct_value_threshold/parallel are accepted and used only │
│                          │ as bounded heuristics/caps.                                                               │
