│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_prefix_cache_reorder                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The original program fails validation for multiple reasons: (1) it returns an empty       │
│                          │ per-row column-orderings list, but the API requires valid per-row orderings consistent    │
│                          │ with the returned data; (2) it mutates values via `df["original_index"]` and              │
│                          │ `merging_columns`, altering the stored data and serialization; (3) its recursive          │
│                          │ group-value logic relies on broken `itertuples` attribute lookups and `Counter` of        │
│                          │ unhashable mixed values, causing exceptions or silent data corruption; (4) it optimizes   │
│                          │ squared field lengths/adjacent rows rather than the actual character-Trie prefix reuse    │
│                          │ objective.                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: rewrite the editable block with a correct, bounded algorithm that preserves every    │
│                          │ cell value and shape, supports row-specific column orders, and directly targets           │
│                          │ character-prefix sharing:                                                                 │
│                          │ - Serialize every cell once (str, "" for NaN) into a shared matrix; keep column           │
│                          │ factorizations (codes + counts + lengths) for all constructions.                          │
│                          │ - Build up to 3 whole-dataset candidate row-field orderings:                              │
│                          │   A) Global frequency ordering: rank columns by sum(len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1)) with    │
│                          │ deterministic tie-breaks.                                                                 │
│                          │   B) Conditional prefix partition tree: recursively pick the remaining column with the    │
│                          │ best length-weighted repeated-value savings inside each group (integer codes, vectorized  │
│                          │ bincount, bounded depth/width), and give each group its own suffix ordering (row-specific │
│                          │ orders).                                                                                  │
│                          │   C) Stable deterministic original-order baseline (cheap tail).                           │
│                          │ - Score each candidate exactly with the ideal serial-Trie objective: sum of adjacent LCP  │
│                          │ lengths over sorted serialized rows, using a C-speed binary search on prefix-slice        │
│                          │ equality (no per-character Python loops).                                                 │
│                          │ - Return the best candidate's dataframe (object dtype, original rows preserved, original  │
│                          │ index kept) and its per-row column orderings. All parameters of the public `reorder`      │
│                          │ signature are accepted; col_merge is honored semantically by keeping merged columns       │
│                          │ adjacent (no value changes);                                                              │
│                          │ one_way_dep/early_stop/row_stop/col_stop/distinct_value_threshold are accepted with safe  │
│                          │ no-op or bounding semantics. Runtime is bounded by whole-dataset construction limits (no  │
│                          │ per-row pandas calls).                                                                    │
