│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation for structural reasons: it returned an empty       │
│                          │ column_orderings list (so per-row column orderings were missing/invalid), added an        │
│                          │ "original_index" helper column, discarded columns, sorted rows, and reconstructed the     │
│                          │ frame via merge/concat paths that altered shape and cell placement relative to the        │
│                          │ declared (empty) orderings. Its recursive value-grouping permuted cells within rows but   │
│                          │ never reported those permutations.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: replace the algorithm body with a correct, bounded candidate-construction approach.  │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell once (str with NaN→"" for scoring only; stored values untouched).  │
│                          │ 2. Build up to three candidate per-row column orderings: (a) a global frequency-ranked    │
│                          │ order scoring sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)) with deterministic ties, (b) a conditional      │
│                          │ prefix-partition tree using factorized integer codes (bounded depth/candidates/nodes),    │
│                          │ (c) row-independent order ranked by (count-1)*len as a differing tradeoff. one_way_dep    │
│                          │ columns are placed immediately after their dependency; col_merge groups are kept          │
│                          │ contiguous.                                                                               │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate exactly with the ideal trie objective (sort serialized rows, sum  │
│                          │ adjacent LCP via C-speed binary-search prefix slicing).                                   │
│                          │ 4. Return the best candidate: the original DataFrame with each row's cells permuted       │
│                          │ according to that row's ordering, with matching per-row column-name orderings. No         │
│                          │ rows/columns added or removed, values identical, object dtype preserved.                  │
