│ patch_name               │ serial_trie_lcp_reorder_fix                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The original program failed validation because its recursive path returned an empty       │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list (`return final_result_df, []`), gave no valid per-row column      │
│                          │ permutations, mutated/rebuilt DataFrames with mismatched column names (e.g. renaming the  │
│                          │ last column to "original_index"), relied on `itertuples` attribute lookups that break     │
│                          │ with non-identifier/duplicate column names, and printed debug output. The whole approach  │
│                          │ also optimized the wrong objective (squared field lengths / adjacent grouping) instead of │
│                          │ character-level Trie prefix reuse.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the editable block with a self-contained, correct implementation that    │
│                          │ preserves all data and shape:                                                             │
│                          │ - Serializes each cell once (missing → "", otherwise `str(v)`) and reuses these strings   │
│                          │ across all candidate constructions.                                                       │
│                          │ - Builds up to three cheap candidate per-row column orderings: (1) a global frequency     │
│                          │ ordering ranked by Σ len(v)·count·(count−1), (2) a bounded conditional partition tree     │
│                          │ (choose a leading field by length-weighted pair repetition, partition rows by its value,  │
│                          │ order suffixes separately per group, bounded depth/group size/candidate columns), and (3) │
│                          │ an alternative frequency/length tradeoff ordering.                                        │
│                          │ - Scores each candidate with the exact ideal serial-Trie objective (sum of adjacent LCPs  │
│                          │ of the sorted serialized rows, computed with C-speed binary-search prefix-slice           │
│                          │ comparisons), and returns the best.                                                       │
│                          │ - Honors `col_merge` (merged columns kept adjacent) and `one_way_dep` (dependent column   │
│                          │ placed right after its dependency) as ordering constraints, uses `col_stop`/`row_stop` as │
│                          │ recursion-depth bounds and `distinct_value_threshold` to bar overly-distinct columns from │
│                          │ being partition fields, and returns valid per-row column orderings consistent with the    │
│                          │ returned DataFrame (whose columns are placed in the modal row ordering as a hedge). No    │
│                          │ data values are altered, added, dropped, or fabricated.                                   │
