╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ fix_global_lcp_column_reorder                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The original program was fundamentally broken for the scoring objective: it returned an   │
│                          │ empty `column_orderings` list (`return final_df, []`), ran a slow, incorrect recursion    │
│                          │ that shuffled cell values across columns (breaking row identity / per-row consistency     │
│                          │ with any ordering), inserted a fabricated `original_index` column, printed debug output,  │
│                          │ and relied on `getattr(row, col)` attribute lookups on itertuples that fail for column    │
│                          │ names with spaces/slashes (visible in the printed column indexes like                     │
│                          │ `review/profileName`). Since the evaluator serializes each output row in the DataFrame's  │
│                          │ fixed column order, only a single global column order actually affects the character-Trie │
│                          │ hit rate (row insertion order is irrelevant for a fixed multiset of strings).             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the whole editable block with a correct, bounded implementation:         │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each column exactly like the evaluator (`fillna("")` semantics via mask on   │
│                          │ `astype(str)`).                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. Build at most three cheap deterministic global column-order candidates: (a)            │
│                          │ pair-repetition gain `sum len(v)*count*(count-1)` descending, (b) distinct-count          │
│                          │ ascending with length tie-break, (c) a different length/frequency tradeoff `sum           │
│                          │ len(v)*count` over repeated values.                                                       │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the true objective — sum of adjacent LCPs of the sorted      │
│                          │ serialized row strings (C-speed `os.path.commonprefix`) divided by total characters — and │
│                          │ return the best.                                                                          │
│                          │ 4. Return the DataFrame with the winning column permutation (all original values          │
│                          │ preserved, object dtype kept for mixed types) and `column_orderings =  * n_rows`,         │
│                          │ guaranteeing orderings are valid permutations consistent with the returned data.          │
