│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_with_lcp_scored_candidates                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The failed program had multiple correctness bugs: it relied on `itertuples` attribute     │
│                          │ access with arbitrary/space-containing column names, mutated data via                     │
│                          │ `group.iloc[...]`/`merge`/`sort_values` (breaking row identity), returned an empty        │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list (invalid per-row orderings), appended an `original_index` column, │
│                          │ and its recursive Counter/`stack` logic crashed or corrupted data on mixed-type frames.   │
│                          │ Root cause: the algorithm attempted value-based row/column recursion with fragile pandas  │
│                          │ operations and never produced valid per-row column orderings.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: complete rewrite preserving the public API. It (1) serializes each cell once with    │
│                          │ exact fillna/astype(str) semantics, (2) factorizes columns to integer codes, (3) builds   │
│                          │ up to three cheap candidate orderings — global frequency-ranked order (score = sum        │
│                          │ len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1)), a bounded conditional partition tree producing per-row orders (depth │
│                          │ bounded by col_stop/row_stop, savings threshold via early_stop), and an average-length    │
│                          │ order, (4) scores each candidate with the exact serial-Trie objective (sum of adjacent    │
│                          │ LCPs of sorted serialized rows, using C-speed binary-search prefix compare), (5) returns  │
│                          │ the best candidate's DataFrame with original columns/index and consistent per-row column  │
│                          │ orderings. col_merge groups are kept adjacent and one_way_dep pairs kept in order in      │
│                          │ every row ordering; data values, shape, and row identity are fully preserved (object      │
│                          │ dtype output).                                                                            │
