│ patch_name               │ per_row_orderings_trie_reuse                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program violated the required API and data-preservation constraints: it      │
│                          │ returned an empty `column_orderings` list (no per-row column orderings), printed debug    │
│                          │ output to stdout, mutated the caller's DataFrame, added an "original_index" helper        │
│                          │ column, dropped/altered rows via filtering, and reshaped data through merges/sorts that   │
│                          │ could lose or duplicate cells — causing validation failure. The root cause is that the    │
│                          │ old recursive value-grouping approach never produced the required per-row column          │
│                          │ permutations.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the algorithm with a clean, bounded construction-selection approach:     │
│                          │ 1. Serialize every cell once (missing → "", mixed types → str), preserving all stored     │
│                          │ values in the returned DataFrame (object dtype).                                          │
│                          │ 2. Build column groups honoring `col_merge` (merged columns kept adjacent — no cell       │
│                          │ values are changed).                                                                      │
│                          │ 3. Construct up to three cheap candidate per-row column orderings: (a) global ordering    │
│                          │ ranked by length-weighted pair repetition `len(v)*cnt*(cnt-1)`, (b) a frequency-first     │
│                          │ variant, and (c) a conditional partition-tree that picks a leading group per row subset   │
│                          │ based on the same length-weighted repetition, with bounded depth and a deterministic      │
│                          │ tail.                                                                                     │
│                          │ 4. Score each candidate with the exact ideal Trie reuse objective (sum of adjacent LCPs   │
│                          │ of sorted serialized rows, using binary-search LCP with C-speed slice comparisons), and   │
│                          │ return the best.                                                                          │
│                          │ 5. Return the DataFrame with all original rows/values in original row order, plus a       │
│                          │ consistent per-row list of column orderings. No stdout output, no input mutation, no      │
│                          │ fabricated or dropped cells.                                                              │
