│ patch_description        │ The original program failed because its recursive group/partition logic produced          │
│                          │ inconsistent output: it returned an empty `column_orderings` list, fabricated an          │
│                          │ "original_index" column that was dropped/re-merged inconsistently, mutated DataFrames     │
│                          │ during recursion (losing/duplicating rows), applied a final global `sort_values` that     │
│                          │ broke per-row column orderings entirely, and its thread-based `column_recursion` wrote    │
│                          │ rows via `as_completed` into positional indices (scrambling row identity). The result     │
│                          │ could not pass the API contract: valid per-row column orderings consistent with returned  │
│                          │ data, preserved shape/values.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the buggy recursion with a correct, bounded construction-selection       │
│                          │ algorithm targeting the real objective (serial character-Trie reuse, measured exactly via │
│                          │ sorted-string adjacent LCP sums):                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Serialize each cell exactly as the evaluator does (NaN → "", else str(value)), reused  │
│                          │ across all candidates via precomputed per-column string arrays and factorized integer     │
│                          │ codes.                                                                                    │
│                          │ 2. Build up to three cheap per-row column-ordering candidates:                            │
│                          │    - Global frequency-ranked ordering: columns sorted by                                  │
│                          │ sum(len(v)*count(v)*(count(v)-1)) with deterministic tie-breaks.                          │
│                          │    - Recursive conditional grouping: within each row group, pick the remaining column     │
│                          │ with the best length-weighted pair repetition (using precomputed codes, bounded depth,    │
│                          │ bounded candidate columns), partition rows on its value, and order suffixes per group.    │
│                          │    - Identity (original column order) as a safe fallback.                                 │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate with the exact ideal-Trie objective: sort serialized rows, sum    │
│                          │ binary-searched LCPs of adjacent strings (slice-equality comparisons in C, no             │
│                          │ per-character Python loops).                                                              │
│                          │ 4. Return the best candidate's per-row permutation applied to the original cell values    │
│                          │ (object dtype, values untouched), with a matching `column_orderings` list of per-row      │
│                          │ column names. `col_merge` still delegates to the parent `merging_columns` when available, │
│                          │ and resulting merged column names are used consistently in the orderings.                 │
