│ patch_description        │ The previous program was fundamentally broken: it fabricated an "original_index" column,  │
│                          │ dropped/altered data via merges and groupby updates, returned an empty column_orderings   │
│                          │ list (the API requires valid per-row orderings consistent with the returned data), and    │
│                          │ its recursive row/column recursion produced inconsistent shapes and assertion-prone code  │
│                          │ paths (visible in the stray prints and failing validations).                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix replaces the entire editable block with a clean, correct implementation:          │
│                          │ 1. Preserve every cell value exactly; only the per-row column permutation changes.        │
│                          │ 2. Build three cheap candidate orderings: (a) global column order ranked by               │
│                          │ sum(len(v)*n*(n-1)), (b) a variant ranked by len(v)*(n-1), (c) a conditional partition    │
│                          │ tree that selects a leading column per group using length-weighted pair repetition        │
│                          │ (count*(count-1)) and gives row-specific suffix orders.                                   │
│                          │ 3. Score each candidate exactly by the ideal serial character-Trie objective: sort the    │
│                          │ fully serialized rows and sum adjacent longest-common-prefix lengths (chunked C-level     │
│                          │ string comparison).                                                                       │
│                          │ 4. Return the best candidate's DataFrame (object dtype, values permuted per row) plus     │
│                          │ matching per-row column orderings; col_merge groups are forced to stay contiguous in      │
│                          │ every row ordering.                                                                       │
