│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ rewrite_prefix_caching_reorder                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program failed validation for multiple structural reasons: (1) it never      │
│                          │ produced valid per-row `column_orderings` (always returned `[]`); (2) the recursive       │
│                          │ `itertuples`/attribute-based row manipulation corrupted/mixed rows, reset indices,        │
│                          │ appended an `original_index` column, dropped and merged columns, and finally re-sorted    │
│                          │ rows — so the returned DataFrame lost row identity and cell alignment; (3) the old        │
│                          │ squared-field-length heuristic (len**2) does not match the actual objective               │
│                          │ (character-prefix Trie reuse, measurable as the sum of LCPs between lexicographically     │
│                          │ adjacent serialized rows). The fix is a full rewrite: serialize each cell exactly as the  │
│                          │ evaluator does (missing -> "", otherwise str), construct at most three cheap              │
│                          │ whole-program candidates — (a) a global frequency-ranked column ordering using            │
│                          │ sum(len(v)*count*(count-1)), (b) a bounded conditional partition tree that gives per-row  │
│                          │ orderings by recursively choosing the length-weighted best repeated field, and (c) the    │
│                          │ original column order as a safe fallback — then select the winner by actually computing   │
│                          │ ideal Trie reuse via sorting the serialized row strings and summing adjacent LCP lengths  │
│                          │ (binary-search prefix comparison, C-speed). All cell values, rows, and columns are        │
│                          │ preserved exactly; col_merge groups are kept contiguous as ordered units; the returned    │
│                          │ DataFrame keeps its original column order and index, and `column_orderings` contains one  │
│                          │ valid ordering per row consistent with the returned data.                                 │
