│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ merge_atomic_tree_multi_base                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The rewrite keeps the exact serial-Trie objective (sorted-string adjacent LCP sum) as the │
│                          │ selector, but improves both construction quality and runtime:                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Merge groups become atomic: after `merging_columns` produces merged columns, the       │
│                          │ algorithm treats each resulting column as an indivisible unit for scoring and splitting   │
│                          │ (merged columns are already single columns, so both the global orders and the conditional │
│                          │ tree only ever split on whole merge groups — no per-row post-hoc merge forcing that could │
│                          │ break contiguity).                                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. The conditional partition tree is now factorized: every column's serialized values are │
│                          │ converted once to integer codes with per-code string lengths. Inside each tree node,      │
│                          │ candidate columns are scored with `np.bincount` over codes (length-weighted pair          │
│                          │ repetition `len*n*(n-1)`), and rows are partitioned by code. No pandas `value_counts`,    │
│                          │ `fillna`, or per-character Python loops inside nodes.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. Bounded construction set of at most four whole-program candidates: global frequency    │
│                          │ order, global length order, tree seeded with the frequency order, and tree seeded with    │
│                          │ the length order (only when they differ). Each is scored exactly once with the real LCP   │
│                          │ objective (binary-search LCP on C-speed slice comparisons), and the best is emitted. This │
│                          │ gives the weakest datasets a second, different tree hypothesis without unbounded search.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Data preservation is unchanged: original cell values are only permuted per row;           │
│                          │ serialization is used solely for scoring. All API parameters are honored with the same    │
│                          │ semantics.                                                                                │
